{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_96","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed the full research project.\n\n- Paper: [main.pdf](/workspaces/detect-different-lies-4bf7-zeroshot-codex-gpt-5-6-sol/paper_draft/main.pdf)\n- LaTeX: [main.tex](/workspaces/detect-different-lies-4bf7-zeroshot-codex-gpt-5-6-sol/paper_draft/main.tex)\n- Reproduction guide: [README.md](/workspaces/detect-different-lies-4bf7-zeroshot-codex-gpt-5-6-sol/README.md)\n- Code: [src/](/workspaces/detect-different-lies-4bf7-zeroshot-codex-gpt-5-6-sol/src)\n- Raw and derived results: [results/](/workspaces/detect-different-lies-4bf7-zeroshot-codex-gpt-5-6-sol/results)\n\nMain findings:\n\n- Only 17/129 known questions produced prompted lies.\n- Of 116 false incentive-condition outputs, 14.7% were clean lies and 74.1% were low-knowledge errors.\n- A conventional condition probe achieved AUROC 1.00, but was already perfect before answer generation—demonstrating prompt leakage.\n- With identical pressure prompts, the answer-state probe distinguished lies from same-pressure honest answers at AUROC 0.959 and lies from hallucinations at 0.896.\n- The hallucination probe performed well on known-versus-unknown answers, but did not reliably distinguish hallucinations from lies: AUROC 0.604, 95% CI [0.475, 0.733].\n\nAll 1,680 generations, 720 activation records, grading decisions, fold assignments, probe scores, figures, and seeds are retained."}}
